       ]
      },
      {
       "id": "y",
       "accepted": [
        "6"
       ]
      }
     ]
    },
    "explanation": "$x^2 - 2x + 3 = x + 3$ gives $x^2 - 3x = x(x - 3) = 0$. $P$ is $(0, 3)$ and $Q$ is $(3, 6)$."
   },
   {
    "label": "(b)",
    "type": "EXTENDED_RESPONSE",
    "marks": 2,
    "markingMode": "MANUAL",
    "stem": "Show that the area of the region enclosed by the line and the curve is $\\displaystyle\\int_0^3 (3x - x^2)\\,dx$.",
    "answerSpec": {
     "markScheme": {
      "points": [
       {
        "id": "p1",
        "description": "M1 identifies the line as the upper boundary for $0 < x < 3$ (e.g. at $x = 1$: $4 > 2$).",
        "marks": 1
       },
       {
        "id": "p2",
        "description": "A1 forms $(x + 3) - (x^2 - 2x + 3) = 3x - x^2$ with limits 0 and 3.",
        "marks": 1
       }
      ]
     },
     "modelAnswer": "For $0 < x < 3$ the line is above the curve (at $x = 1$ the line gives 4 and the curve 2). Area $= \\int_0^3 \\left[(x + 3) - (x^2 - 2x + 3)\\right]dx = \\int_0^3 (3x - x^2)\\,dx$."
    },
    "explanation": "Upper minus lower between the intersection points."
   },
   {
